Z||000000||camon||10-04-2001||02:34 AM||||Hey all!<P>I posted last week about the 87 fiero GT I bought for 1000.00 and it had a few things wrong with it (wheel bearing and a cold engine stall problem and cold engine hestition while accelerating). Well i'm happy to report that the Engine hesitation and stall problems have been corrected I replaced the plugs and wires along with the O2 sensor and used injector cleaner and it runs fine now. I also jacked the car to examine the wheel bearings (by wiggling the wheels). It seems as though both fronts wiggle so i'm assuming both need replaced. I have price searched them (the inner and outer wheel bearings) and was amazed how cheap they are 6.99 for the inner and 8.99 for the outer and 2.99 for the seal) Is this all that is needed for the job????? 2 bearings (inner and outer) and a seal for each side? Anything else needed?????<P>Also a new problem surfaced....nothing major but does seem like a pain to get to. The water pump has gone bad. Driving it last sunday and I heard a whirrling noise from the engine and then the temp guage went through the roof. Left it cool and got it home. Examined the pump and there was loads of play in the water pump pulley.......so I guess my assumption is correct and the water pump has gone south....does this sound correct?? Also any body do a pump job on thier fiero and have any heads up calls on the job (stuff to remember and to watch for)???<P>Still haven't had the time to take those snap shots of it....I'll take them as soon as I get the pump and bearings fixed!<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Kasdan||10-04-2001||09:42 AM||||Get a pump from autozone, like 20 bucks at most if i remember correctly.  it comes with a gasket, so all you ned is the blue sealer.  My job was a little harder since i had to remove my power steering pump to get to the water pump, but that was no great feat.  lightly sand the block where the pump goes to remove all excess gasket/seater, and liberally apply sealer ONLY to the spots that will have the gasket, try not to get any into the block....<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero21.jpg">         <BR>Power Steering, Holley Scoop<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||10-04-2001||02:56 PM||||The front wheel bearings may just need to be adjusted.  The procedure is:<BR>Tighten the wheel bearing nut to 12 ft/lbs while rotating the disc in the forward direction,<BR>Back the nut off until it is slightly loose,<BR>Retighten the nut BY HAND (no wrench) until it is snug,<BR>Back it out as needed to put the cotter pin in.<BR>When the bearings are properly adjusted you may feel a tiny amount of play when you rock the tire back and forth.  It's important that you don't tighten them too tight or they will wear very quickly.  Might be a good time to repack them with grease too.  The Haynes book has a good procedure for this.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||dman||10-04-2001||04:11 PM||||Hello everyone.<P>Welcome to the forum camon. When changing your water pump make sure you get one that has a metal impeller (not plastic, those are widely believed to be junk). As mentioned earlier Autozone can sell you one for 20 bucks (GMBH brand or something like that). The ones I've bought had metal impellers, but others who have purchased from Autozone ended up with plastic. Just look and you'll know.<P>Note: There are numerous bolts and a couple of studs holding down the pump. I find it useful to remove them and poke them into cardboard in the proper pattern so that there is no confusion during re-installation.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||camon||10-04-2001||10:06 PM||||Thanks for the input on the water pump and wheel bearings. Will do the jobs this saturay (I will also make sure that the bearings are bad and not just out of adjustment. I already bought the pump and it has metal impellers. Paid 24.99 at autozone. I also got the bearings and seal seeing they were so cheap (thought i'd get them..... even if the bearings just need adjusted....this way I have them for the future).<P>Again thank you guys for the advise and also the other guys in the other topic I started for the advise on the stall and hesitiation problem. Don't know what it was (plugs, wires, O2 sensor, or clugged injectors or a combination of a few or all) I changed them all at the same time and it starts and runn great now....so thank you for that advice.<P>well keep you posted....later on!||||reg||1
